  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nCurrently have a deep upper trough extending from Quebec down \nthrough the Mid-Atlantic states. Meanwhile out west, one upper \nimpulse is moving into southwest TX with a stronger piece of energy \ntracking southeast out of the Central Great Basin and approaching the \nDesert Southwest. At the surface,, large area of high pressure \nextends from western Ontario through the Mississippi Valley. \n\nSurface high will shift east today allowing for return flow to setup \nacross the High Plains. Showers will move into western KS by late \nthis afternoon as 850-700mb moisture transport increases. Still \nlooking for the upper wave to close off over the TX Panhandle \ntonight and then sink southeast along the Red River on Sunday. As \nthe more widespread precip works east tonight, rain will start to \nshift southeast as the upper low does. So we are currently expecting \nthe southwest portion of our forecast area to have the higher \nrainfall chances tonight through Sunday. By Sun night, the bulk of \nthe rain chances will be south of the forecast area in Oklahoma. \n\nUpper impulse will be tracking across the Arklatex region by Mon \nmorning with another western CONUS trough just off of the CA coast. \nThis will leave upper ridging in place across the Plains to start to \nwork week with increasing temps. Western CONUS trough will remain \nvery progressive and by Tue evening will be lifting out of the \nCentral Great Basin and into the Central Plains on Wed. Lee \ntroughing will deepen ahead of this wave and will setup strong south \nwinds for both Tue and especially Wed with some gusts over 40 mph \nlikely for areas along and west of I-135. While some showers will be \npossible across eastern KS on Wed, not looking for a widespread \nprecip event with this system. This impulse will rapidly lift \nnortheast and will be moving into the western Great Lakes region by \nThu afternoon.",
